The '''DIKW pyramid''' is a conceptual model that distinguishes data, information, knowledge and wisdom. It often depicts data as the bottom layer and wisdom as the top. The diagram is used to discuss how recorded observations can become useful for understanding and decisions.

== Intellectual history ==
Ackoff's 1989 account is a prominent formulation. Rowley's 2007 review compared definitions and transformations across textbooks and found varying accounts; Frické (2009) criticized the pyramid's assumptions and offered alternative conceptions. It is a pedagogical and management framework, not a law of cognition.

== Distinguishing transformations ==
A possible workflow is observation → record → contextual interpretation → justified claim → decision. Each arrow requires assumptions: units, measurement validity, background knowledge, evidence, goals and values. A database record may already encode a theory, while a domain expert can recognize a pattern before formal data collection. Knowledge-how may be tacit and is not necessarily produced by aggregating records. Wisdom is a normative and practical concept rather than a computable monotonic quantity.

== Evaluation in an information system ==
For an environmental monitoring workflow, document the sensor calibration, metadata, processing code, inferential model and uncertainty separately from the action decision. Test the accuracy of reported observations, predictive validity of explanations and consequences of the decision against independent benchmarks. Such checks can improve a workflow but do not test whether there is a single universal DIKW ordering. No quantitative conversion from Shannon bits to wisdom follows from the diagram.

